<p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/blogbook-ghost-header-rb.7540.jpg" width="800" height="226" style="margin:0 auto;clear:both;" class="header" /></p>
<p>I&rsquo;ve been putting a lot of effort into <a href="https://blogbook.my">BlogBook</a>, my new app for creating archival books from years of blog posts. I have it thoroughly tested with WordPress, and complete with Micro.blog, but I have yet to find someone with a Ghost blog who can run that integration through its paces.</p>

<p>There&rsquo;s no such thing as an app that will convert every blog in the world to a perfect book, but I think BlogBook gets pretty darn close. With the blogs I&rsquo;ve tested, it&rsquo;s created EPUBs like a champ with a wide array of questionable markup dating back a decade or two.</p>

<p>Most recently, I added a title page editor and cover generator. Provide it with a logo, add a background image, and let it insert title and author on the cover, or design your own cover and provide a flattened image. Then you can edit the title page in Markdown, adding any headshots, dedications, or whatever content you like.</p>


              
              <p><img src="https://cdn3.brettterpstra.com/uploads/2026/11/blogbook-title-page-800.jpg"></p>
              
              <figcaption>Title page editor</figcaption>
            

<p>The result is a lovingly-crafted EPUB, ready for sharing, sale, or just archiving your years of writing. It can also output to Markdown, and with an app like <a href="https://markedapp.com/" title="Markdown Preview for Mac">Marked 3</a>, you can get just about any output format you could want.</p>

<p>Allison Sheridan has been kind enough to let me test against the huge catalog of the <a href="https://www.podfeet.com/blog/">Podfeet blog</a>, and has riddled me with suggestions that have made the app way better.</p>

<p>Thomas Zahrgave me access to years of <a href="https://micro.blog/crumblingbiscuit">Micro.blog posts</a> for testing against, and it raised a bunch of issues that I wouldn&rsquo;t have come up with just testing WordPress.</p>

<p>I offer Ghost support, and in my very surface-level testing it works well, but I really need at least one Ghost blogger to stress test it and let me know where I can make it better. I&rsquo;ve asked around on Mastodon to no avail&hellip; do I really not know any Ghost users?</p>

<p>If you have a Ghost blog with 100+ posts, I&rsquo;d love to hear from you. <a href="https://brettterpstra.com/contact/">Contact me directly</a>, and let&rsquo;s work together! Obviously, there&rsquo;s a free copy of <a href="https://blogbook.my">BlogBook</a> in it for you. I&rsquo;m still open to WordPress and Micro.blog testers, and I&rsquo;m focused on Ghost at the moment, but if you have either of the other two and want to help out, feel free to contact me as well.</p>

